Ladies and Gentlemen,
Welcome to today’s conversation — a topic that touches almost every heart in the modern world:

Why does love feel harder than ever before?
And more importantly… what can we do about it?

Once upon a time, love was about survival, security, and tradition.
People stayed together out of duty, expectation — or simply because they had no choice.

But today…
We don’t just want a partner.
We want a best friend, a soulmate, a co-parent, a therapist, a financial partner, and a passionate lover — all wrapped into one.

That’s not love. That’s pressure.

Modern love is full of possibilities — but also paralyzed by choice.
We swipe endlessly, compare constantly, and wonder:
"Is this the right one?"
"What if someone better is out there?"

And in this endless scroll for perfection — we forget:
No relationship can be perfect… but it can be deep, meaningful, and real.

We expect love to complete us — instead of challenge us to grow.
When discomfort arises, we think something is wrong.
But often, that discomfort is the birthplace of true intimacy.

As Esther Perel says:

"The quality of our relationships determines the quality of our lives."

So, what can we do?

We need to shift the question from
“Am I with the right person?”
to
“Can I be the right person in this relationship?”

Because love is not something you find — it’s something you create.
Day by day.
Moment by moment.
Through presence, patience, and emotional courage.

It’s not easy.
But it’s worth it.

So, if love feels hard right now…
Maybe it’s not broken.
Maybe… it’s just real.

Modern relationships are shaped by a profound shift in how we view love, partnership, and commitment. Unlike the past, where relationships were often built on obligation, tradition, or societal expectations, today’s love is rooted in choice and personal freedom. This means we are no longer forced to stay in relationships out of survival, dependency, or family pressure—we stay only if we feel fulfilled, emotionally supported, and truly connected. While this freedom is empowering, it also introduces a layer of complexity that past generations didn’t have to navigate. When love is a choice, every day becomes a subtle decision to stay. People want to be wanted, not needed. They want a partner who chooses them out of desire, not duty. This sounds ideal, but it also means the foundation of the relationship is constantly tested by emotional fluctuations, personal growth, and changing expectations. Without the structure of obligation, relationships must be held together by continuous emotional labor, clear communication, and mutual commitment, not just social contracts. When people have the freedom to leave at any time, they often question whether staying is the right decision, especially during hard times. Discomfort, conflict, or boredom are no longer accepted as normal phases—they are often seen as signs of incompatibility or failure. And so, instead of working through the complexity, many choose to exit, believing that love should always feel easy, passionate, and emotionally perfect. This creates pressure to maintain an ideal relationship image at all times, which is unrealistic. Love requires vulnerability, resilience, and acceptance of imperfection, but freedom can trick us into believing we shouldn’t have to compromise or struggle. It promotes the illusion that there is always something better out there—another partner, another experience, another version of life that feels more exciting or less complicated. This mindset leads to comparison, dissatisfaction, and a lack of long-term emotional investment. Moreover, freedom demands self-awareness and emotional maturity. We are expected to know what we want, communicate it clearly, and take responsibility for our personal healing—all while building a relationship with another person doing the same. This emotional independence is beautiful but also exhausting. We are now responsible for managing our own happiness and helping our partner feel secure, seen, and supported without losing ourselves in the process. In traditional relationships, roles were predefined, which gave a sense of order, even if it limited personal growth. In modern relationships, roles are negotiated and redefined repeatedly. Each couple must create their own rules, their own boundaries, and their own path. There is no one-size-fits-all template, and that flexibility, while freeing, also demands more emotional effort and intention. We now expect our partners to be not only loving and loyal but also emotionally intelligent, self-aware, and consistently evolving. We want stability and passion, safety and excitement, freedom and deep connection—all at once. These dual needs often conflict with each other and require delicate balancing. It’s not easy to maintain independence while fostering intimacy or to be emotionally vulnerable while protecting personal space. The very freedom we value can sometimes make us feel disconnected, uncertain, or misunderstood. But that doesn’t mean love is dying—it means it’s evolving. The complexity of modern love is not a flaw, it’s a reflection of our growth as individuals and as a society. To thrive in this new model, we must learn to embrace uncertainty, to stay curious about each other, and to see love not as a destination but as a dynamic process. Freedom in relationships is not about doing whatever we want—it’s about choosing each other every day with full awareness of what that choice means. When done consciously, that choice becomes more powerful than any obligation ever could.

In the landscape of modern love, the expectations we place on our partners have grown heavier and more complex than ever before. No longer is a relationship simply about companionship or building a life together. Today, we desire one person to be our entire emotional ecosystem. We want them to be our romantic partner who brings passion and desire, our best friend who shares laughs and secrets, our therapist who listens to our past traumas and helps us heal, our motivational coach who pushes us to become our best selves, and our constant emotional anchor who understands every mood without us having to explain it. This ideal, while deeply romanticized, is nearly impossible to fulfill and places overwhelming pressure on even the most loving relationships. No one person can be everything all the time, and expecting that creates disappointment not because our partners are failing us but because our expectations are unrealistic. The modern world tells us that love should complete us, that we should find our other half, but in doing so, we forget that two halves do not make a whole when they are both looking for fulfillment outside themselves. This leads to emotional dependency where instead of bringing our own joy and stability to a relationship, we demand it from another. When our partner fails to respond perfectly to our needs, we feel let down, and this can spiral into resentment, disconnection, or the illusion that the relationship is broken when in reality it is simply human. Real love is about complementing each other, not completing each other. By trying to extract all forms of emotional support, intimacy, entertainment, validation, and healing from one person, we actually shrink the space of the relationship. Instead of allowing love to breathe and grow, it becomes a performance measured by how well each person meets the other’s every expectation. This removes the freedom to be imperfect, to fail sometimes, to simply not have the answers. A partner can love you deeply and still not be equipped to handle all of your emotional needs. They may not have the tools to help you process childhood wounds or career anxiety. They may not always be in the mood to talk things through at length or hold space for your every breakdown. That doesn’t mean they don’t care. It means they’re human. What we often overlook is that in older times, people had entire communities to turn to. Friends, family, elders, and even spiritual leaders played roles in emotional support. Now, with more isolated lifestyles and digital lives, we’ve collapsed all these roles into the expectation that one person should fulfill them all. That’s not love—that’s pressure. And pressure kills connection. When someone feels they are always falling short of your needs, they begin to pull away. When they feel judged for not responding the way you hoped, they stop trying. Love turns from a source of warmth into a place of silent tension. Instead, we must embrace the truth that it’s okay—healthy even—to have multiple sources of emotional support. We should turn to friends for companionship, therapists for healing, and hobbies for joy. Our partner should be one beautiful, evolving part of a bigger emotional world, not the entire world. When we reduce our expectations and see each other through a lens of compassion rather than performance, we create space for deeper connection. We allow love to be what it is—messy, imperfect, but real. And real love doesn’t mean being everything for someone. It means being enough, just as we are, without having to carry the entire emotional weight of another’s life. That is where true intimacy begins—not in perfection, but in presence.

In today’s digital age, the sheer volume of choices we have when it comes to relationships has created a new kind of emotional challenge that previous generations never had to face. With a simple swipe, we can access hundreds or thousands of potential partners, each one presented with carefully selected photos, witty bios, and a curated version of their best self. This abundance of choice, while seemingly empowering, often leads to emotional paralysis and deep dissatisfaction. The human mind, though capable of handling complex thoughts, struggles when faced with too many options. This is known as the paradox of choice: the more options we have, the harder it becomes to make a decision, and the more we second-guess the choices we do make. In relationships, this looks like people who are constantly searching, comparing, hesitating, and leaving. Instead of feeling grateful for a good connection, we often wonder if there’s someone out there who could offer just a bit more—more beauty, more emotional intelligence, more success, more passion. The moment we hit a rough patch in a relationship, our minds quickly shift to the idea that maybe we’ve chosen the wrong person. Instead of working through difficulties, we consider alternatives. We compare our current partner to others, sometimes not even real people but imaginary ideals influenced by movies, social media, and dating apps. This constant comparing keeps us emotionally detached. We don’t fully invest because we’re always mentally evaluating, always half-in, half-out. And when we don’t fully invest, we don’t experience the true depth of love. Real connection and satisfaction come from time, patience, shared growth, and emotional effort—none of which are possible when we’re stuck in a state of indecision or constantly looking over our shoulder. Emotional paralysis happens when we become so overwhelmed by possibilities that we can’t commit to any. We fear making the wrong choice, so we avoid choosing altogether or keep our options open, hoping clarity will come from the outside rather than from within. This hesitation creates anxiety, dissatisfaction, and confusion. We remain in relationships with one foot out the door or avoid relationships entirely out of fear of regret. We begin to equate freedom with happiness, but in truth, the happiest people are often those who made a conscious choice and poured themselves into it, allowing love to mature and deepen over time. Too many choices also rob us of contentment. In constantly measuring our partner against others, we fail to see them for who they truly are. We overlook their strengths, their unique qualities, and the quiet beauty of their presence. We forget that everyone has flaws and that deep, lasting love isn’t about finding someone perfect—it’s about finding someone whose imperfections we can accept and love through. Dissatisfaction grows when we compare real, imperfect relationships to the filtered lives of others online. We see only highlight reels, never the arguments, insecurities, or silent struggles that exist behind closed doors. This illusion tricks us into believing we’re missing out, that our relationship is lacking something essential. The truth is that satisfaction doesn’t come from the quantity of choices but from the quality of commitment. When we stop chasing better and start appreciating what’s in front of us, we allow love to flourish. We begin to experience the peace that comes with emotional presence, the warmth of shared memories, and the richness of mutual growth. Love becomes less about ideal outcomes and more about shared experiences. Too much choice may seem like freedom, but if it keeps us disconnected, unsatisfied, and paralyzed by indecision, then it’s not freedom at all. Real freedom is the ability to choose and then wholeheartedly live in that choice without constantly wondering what else might have been. That is where true emotional satisfaction lives—not in endless options, but in the courage to choose and the grace to stay.

Discomfort in love is often misunderstood as a signal that something is broken or misaligned, but in reality, it may be a natural part of growth within a relationship. Love, especially deep and lasting love, requires us to show up not just as we are, but as we are becoming. It challenges us to be vulnerable, to communicate honestly, to confront our fears, and to share our imperfections. These processes are rarely comfortable. Growth always comes with discomfort because it pushes us beyond our emotional habits, our past wounds, and our idealized visions of what love is supposed to feel like. When two people truly connect, they inevitably mirror parts of each other that are unhealed or underdeveloped. This mirroring can feel uncomfortable, even painful, because it brings old wounds to the surface. But that discomfort is not a sign that love is wrong. It is often a sign that love is working. A healthy relationship is not one that avoids conflict or discomfort but one that provides a safe space to explore those moments without fear of abandonment or judgment. In fact, some of the most important emotional breakthroughs in a relationship happen during or after moments of discomfort—those tense conversations, misunderstood silences, or moments of emotional distance. They reveal the places where healing is needed, where better understanding must be built, and where deeper intimacy can be formed. Many people today believe that if love is real, it should always feel good. We associate love with ease, flow, and joy. And while those things are certainly part of love, they are not the whole picture. Real love is not always easy; it is often a journey of emotional excavation and rebuilding. When we only chase the feeling of comfort, we risk staying on the surface of connection. We avoid the depth where true emotional intimacy lives. Discomfort invites us to ask hard questions: Why did that comment hurt me? Why do I shut down when I feel criticized? Why do I feel insecure even though my partner reassures me? These questions lead us inward and allow us to grow not only as partners but as individuals. We become more emotionally aware, more compassionate, and more resilient. In a mature relationship, discomfort is not a red flag but a doorway. It opens up conversations that matter. It challenges us to communicate more clearly, to listen more deeply, and to love more consciously. Rather than avoiding the hard parts, growing couples learn to sit with them, talk through them, and move forward together. This doesn’t mean tolerating toxicity or abuse—those are very different from the natural struggles of emotional growth. It means recognizing that no relationship is free from friction, and sometimes the very friction we want to escape is the force that shapes a deeper bond. Discomfort also arises when we evolve individually within a relationship. As we change—our needs, goals, and emotional landscapes—the relationship must adapt. That adaptation can feel shaky, even scary, but it’s also a sign that both people are growing and trying to meet each other in new ways. Staying the same might feel safe, but it can also lead to emotional stagnation. True connection requires ongoing adjustment and renewal, and that means facing change together—even when it feels hard. The discomfort that comes with love is a reflection of our emotional edges being stretched. It is not failure. It is not incompatibility. It is an invitation. When you feel discomfort in love, instead of running from it or labeling the relationship as wrong, consider what it’s asking you to learn or unlearn. Consider whether it’s pointing to something within yourself that’s ready to heal or expand. Love, like any meaningful journey, will challenge you. But if you stay present, stay curious, and stay open, that discomfort can transform into growth, and that growth can deepen your connection in ways comfort never could.
In today’s world of fast connections and even faster disconnections, many people approach relationships with a checklist mindset, constantly evaluating the other person and asking themselves, “Is this the right person for me?” While it’s natural to want compatibility, this question can often become a trap that keeps us focused outward rather than inward. It places all the responsibility for the relationship’s success on whether the other person meets our standards, fulfills our needs, and lives up to our ideals. But real love and lasting relationships are not built only on finding the perfect match; they are built on becoming a good partner. That’s why the more powerful and transformative question is, “Can I be the right person for this relationship?” This shift in perspective changes everything. Instead of analyzing and critiquing the other person’s every action, we begin to reflect on our own behavior, our own triggers, our own patterns of communication, and our willingness to grow. Relationships are not about finding someone who completes you or never disappoints you. They are about two people, both imperfect, learning how to be present, supportive, and emotionally available to each other. When we constantly search for the right person, we risk never settling, because no one will ever be perfect. There will always be flaws, disagreements, moments of boredom, and emotional distance. But when we ask if we can be the right person, we take responsibility for the space we are creating within the relationship. Are we listening without judgment? Are we expressing our needs with kindness? Are we reacting with patience, or from fear and insecurity? These are the questions that foster growth and intimacy. Being the right person doesn’t mean changing your core self to fit someone else’s expectations. It means showing up as your most grounded and compassionate self. It means doing the inner work that allows you to love more fully, to forgive more easily, and to stay present even when things get hard. When both people in a relationship ask this question, the result is a dynamic, evolving partnership where each person is accountable for their role in the connection. Instead of blaming each other when things go wrong, they explore how they might have contributed to the conflict and what they can do differently next time. This kind of emotional maturity builds trust and deepens intimacy. It also softens the unrealistic expectation that our partner should always be a certain way. We begin to accept them as a complex, growing human, just like us. Asking “Can I be the right person?” also helps during periods of doubt or uncertainty. It shifts the focus from judging the relationship to nurturing it. Often, the connection we crave is waiting just beyond a hard conversation or a moment of vulnerability that we’ve been avoiding. By choosing to show up fully and work on ourselves, we create the very relationship we thought we had to find. In the end, love is not about perfection or guarantees. It’s about choosing someone and then choosing to be the best version of ourselves in that relationship. It’s about moving from consumerism to commitment, from entitlement to effort. When we stop trying to find the perfect person and start becoming the kind of person we would want to be with, we stop chasing fantasies and start building something real. The truth is, the right person doesn’t just appear—they emerge through your willingness to love with intention, to grow through difficulty, and to stay when it matters most. The question isn’t always whether they are right for you, but whether you’re willing to be right for them. And when that commitment is mutual, love becomes not just something you find, but something you create.

In the modern age, love has shifted from a passive discovery to an active creation. While older generations often accepted love as something that followed tradition, obligation, or survival, today’s love is built on choice, emotional fulfillment, and self-awareness. Many people still begin the journey of love by searching for the perfect partner, someone who meets all their criteria, shares all their values, and aligns with their dreams without friction. But this pursuit of perfection, influenced by movies, social media, and idealistic narratives, often leads to disappointment and confusion. Real people are not perfect. They come with flaws, past experiences, emotional wounds, and evolving identities. If we wait to find someone who fits an ideal mold, we may never truly experience the richness of love that comes from vulnerability, imperfection, and shared effort. Love today is not a magical match that completes us; it is a process of two people learning how to build trust, connection, and meaning together. This co-creation requires presence, intention, patience, and emotional labor. It asks each person to bring their authentic self while also being open to change, compromise, and mutual growth. The idea of co-creating love shifts the focus from what we can get to what we can give, from what’s missing to what can be built. It removes the pressure of finding “the one” and replaces it with the empowering possibility of becoming the right one for each other through shared experiences. It allows us to embrace the reality that love doesn’t arrive fully formed; it is shaped, day by day, in the small choices we make to show up, communicate honestly, forgive, and try again. This mindset also helps us face the inevitable challenges of long-term connection. In a relationship rooted in perfectionism, every conflict feels like a threat, every disagreement feels like failure, and every moment of doubt becomes a reason to leave. But in a relationship built on co-creation, challenges are seen as invitations to deepen understanding and intimacy. Instead of thinking, “This shouldn’t be happening,” we begin to ask, “What can we learn from this?” or “How can we grow through this together?” That shift builds resilience and keeps love alive beyond the honeymoon phase. Co-creating love also respects individuality. It recognizes that both people in a relationship are on their own personal journeys, with evolving needs, dreams, and emotional landscapes. Rather than merging into one identity or expecting the other to stay the same forever, co-creation allows each person to evolve while still choosing to walk the path together. It is not a fixed destination but a fluid dance between independence and togetherness. This form of love is richer, deeper, and more sustainable because it honors reality. It is not built on fantasy or projection but on mutual commitment and shared values. It allows for grace, for silence, for repair after rupture. It doesn’t demand constant perfection or unbroken happiness—it makes space for sadness, frustration, and uncertainty, knowing that those too are part of love’s tapestry. The beauty of co-created love is that it is truly unique. No two relationships are the same because no two people are the same. Each couple builds their own rituals, language, rhythms, and ways of being. That uniqueness becomes their story, their world, their home. Love, then, becomes less about chasing a fairytale and more about writing a living, breathing story together. It is shaped by attention, care, laughter, effort, and presence. It requires courage to be seen and tenderness to keep seeing each other anew. When we let go of perfection and lean into the process of co-creation, we open ourselves to a kind of love that is not flawless—but deeply real. And in that realness, we find something more enduring than any fantasy: the joy of being chosen again and again, not because we are perfect, but because we are willing to grow, together.
